//! lotus/stereo_limit_env_hold.rye -- ALES169: the ATTACK/HOLD/RELEASE BRICKWALL LIMITER carried into stereo -- the LINKED HELD envelope (ALES165) driving ONE LINKED ceiling (ALES158) over time, the fourth spender of the held
//! time base and the rung that makes the stereo HELD dynamics QUARTET whole. ALES163 carried the breathing limiter into stereo: one shared envelope pinning the linked peak to the ceiling as it crosses and easing off as it
//! falls away, one gain applied to both channels so the image never lurches. Yet an un-held limiter still releases and re-engages on a brief DUCK below the ceiling between two loud passages -- it pumps, and the ear hears the
//! level move across the whole field at once. A real limiter carries a HOLD knob for exactly this: on a fall away from a peak, it pins the peak for a little while before the release begins, so the gain reduction stays engaged
//! through the duck and the passage stays whole. ALES165 built that hold stage INSIDE the linked follower and proved it once. This rung spends that proof: it drives ALES49's ceiling from the LINKED HELD envelope rather than the
//! plain linked one -- the same stereo limiter, now with the patience that keeps it from pumping the image.
//!
//! Two proofs fused, held. The DETECTOR is the linked HELD follower (ALES165): one shared envelope steps toward the linked peak max(|left|, |right|), the ONE shared hold countdown pinning that peak across a fall so the two
//! channels never release on different samples. The GAIN is linked (ALES158): where the held envelope rises above the ceiling the single gain ceil/e (in (0, 1) since 1 <= ceil < e) is applied to BOTH raw samples -- ALES49's
//! ceiling reached over time on the linked held envelope rather than clamped the instant it is crossed. The limiter is the compressor at an infinite ratio: the envelope's target pinned to the ceiling, no ratio knob. The gain
//! math is not re-invented (it is ALES49's ceiling), and the time base is not re-invented (it is ALES165's linked held env_step_hold); this rung is exactly their composition over public APIs, adding only the hold knob to the
//! stereo breathing limiter. It is the stereo mirror of ALES59's held mono limiter.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: refuse BadCeiling (outside [1, sample_max]), BadCoeff (an attack or release outside (0, 1]), BadHold (a hold longer than the widest clip), and BadRange (a span past the shared length) BEFORE any write.
//! Otherwise walk the span one sample-PAIR at a time, advancing ONE i64 envelope with ONE u32 hold countdown toward the linked peak by ALES165's stereo_env_step_hold: envelope within the ceiling leaves the pair playing (both
//! pass byte for byte); envelope above the ceiling applies the one gain ceil/e to both raw samples. On the above-ceiling branch e > ceil >= 1, so the divide is always safe -- the zero-envelope case the gate faced cannot arise.
//!
//! The crux -- six laws. THE ZERO-HOLD LAW (the crux link): a hold of zero makes the linked held follower the plain linked follower, so this equals ALES163's stereo_limit_env byte for byte on both -- the held rung's no-hold
//! limit is exactly the un-held rung already proven. THE LINKED-GAIN-OVER-TIME LAW: a left-always-peak master has left' equal to ALES59's mono limit_env_hold(left) byte for byte, the right scaled by the same fraction ceil/e,
//! the independent per-channel limit_env_hold genuinely differing. THE IMAGE LAW: the shared gain preserves the L:R ratio, an out-of-phase pair returning still out of phase, no magnitude expanding, no sign flipping. THE KNOB /
//! NO-PUMP LAWS: below the ceiling the identity everywhere, and a NONZERO HOLD keeping the gain reduction engaged across a brief duck (it does not pump, the held magnitude no larger than the un-held one and strictly quieter at
//! the duck). THE CARRY LAW: a span limited in two pieces (the split mid-hold) equals the whole once on both, env AND hold_left carrying. THE SUB-CEILING / SILENCE / BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a master whose linked
//! held envelope never crosses the ceiling passing byte for byte, silence staying silence, left.len == right.len after, each of the four refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, count = 0 the identity on both.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES169.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-073957_fill-ales169-lotus-stereo-limit-env-hold.md
//! Stands on: lotus/stereo_hold_env.rye (ALES165) -- the LINKED HELD follower, its stereo_env_step_hold advancing the one shared envelope with the one shared hold countdown toward the linked peak, reused whole;
//! lotus/stereo_limit_env.rye (ALES163) -- the un-held linked breathing limiter this reduces to at a hold of zero, re-proven beside it as the zero-hold limit; lotus/limit_env_hold.rye (ALES59) -- the mono attack/hold/release
//! limiter, its LimitEnvHoldError reused whole and its output the left-always-peak comparison; lotus/limit.rye (ALES49) -- the ceiling; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) -- the StereoClip; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) -- the Clip, sample_max,
//! max_clip.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) carrying one shared i64 envelope and one shared u32 hold countdown, siloed to lotus/. One linked-peak comparison and at most one
//! multiply-then-divide for the envelope step plus one per channel for the gain, all in i64 (|sample|-ceil <= 32768-32767 never overflowing), the gain ceil/e in (0, 1) so every scaled magnitude is strictly below its input's
//! and fits i16. The ceiling is a magnitude in sample units, the attack, release, and hold counted in sample INDICES rather than milliseconds against a clock. No lookahead, no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network,
//! no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_limit_env_hold.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const envelope = @import("envelope.rye");
const stereo_hold_env = @import("stereo_hold_env.rye");
const stereo_limit_env = @import("stereo_limit_env.rye");
const limit_env_hold = @import("limit_env_hold.rye");
const limit = @import("limit.rye");

pub const stereo_limit_env_hold_version = "20260815.073957";

/// The linked attack/hold/release limiter's faults are exactly ALES59's own `LimitEnvHoldError` -- `BadCeiling`, `BadCoeff`, `BadHold`, `BadRange` -- reused by name, since fusing the linked held detector with the linked ceiling
/// adds no new fault: it validates one shared ceiling, one shared attack, one shared release, one shared hold, and one span, then advances a single held envelope and applies one gain per pair to both channels together. A
/// limiter has no ratio knob (it is the compressor at an infinite ratio), so there is no BadRatio here.
pub const StereoLimitEnvHoldError = limit_env_hold.LimitEnvHoldError;

/// Limit `count` sample-PAIRS starting at `start` in the stereo master `sc` to the magnitude `ceil`, the ONE gain reduction FOLLOWING the LINKED HELD envelope that CONTINUES from the caller's shared `env` and `hold_left` rather
/// than from silence -- the carried-state form (ALES43's lesson), now carrying BOTH the shared envelope and the shared hold countdown so the hold survives across a call boundary. Each pair advances ALES165's linked held follower
/// one step toward the linked peak `max(|left|, |right|)` (attack while rising and rearming the shared hold, the peak pinned while the countdown lasts, release once it expires); where the held envelope sits within the ceiling
/// both channels pass byte for byte, and where it rises above, the single gain `ceil/e` (in (0, 1)) is applied to BOTH raw samples -- one detector, one hold, one ceiling, the image held. Refuses BadCeiling, BadCoeff, BadHold,
/// and BadRange before any write; on refusal `env`, `hold_left`, and both channels are untouched. Values only -- never the length, and the channels leave balanced.
pub fn stereo_limit_hold_follow_carry(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    env: *i64,
    hold_left: *u32,
    ceil: i32,
    attack_num: u32,
    attack_den: u32,
    release_num: u32,
    release_den: u32,
    hold_samples: u32,
) StereoLimitEnvHoldError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced -- the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the shared gain preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    // invariant: the ceiling is a legal magnitude -- at least one sample unit and no greater than the i16 rail. Before any write.
    if (ceil < 1) return error.BadCeiling;
    if (ceil > @as(i32, timeline.sample_max)) return error.BadCeiling;
    // invariant: both attack and release coefficients are legal fractions in (0, 1] -- checked by the follower's one proven rule.
    if (!envelope.coeff_ok(attack_num, attack_den)) return error.BadCoeff;
    if (!envelope.coeff_ok(release_num, release_den)) return error.BadCoeff;
    // invariant: the hold is bounded by the widest clip -- a longer hold could never expire within any span. Before any write.
    if (hold_samples > timeline.max_clip) return error.BadHold;
    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the SHARED length -- checked once, without underflow, so it holds identically for both channels and a refusal never limits one channel and leaves the other torn.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    if (start > len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > len - start) return error.BadRange;

    const cl: i64 = @as(i64, ceil);
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < count) : (i += 1) {
        const xl: i16 = sc.left.buf[start + i];
        const xr: i16 = sc.right.buf[start + i];
        // Advance the ONE shared linked HELD follower toward the pair's linked peak (ALES165's proven step); e is the smoothed-and-held linked magnitude, in [0, sample_max].
        _ = stereo_hold_env.stereo_env_step_hold(env, hold_left, xl, xr, attack_num, attack_den, release_num, release_den, hold_samples);
        const e: i64 = env.*;
        // invariant: the linked held envelope stays a legal magnitude -- never negative, never past the rail (the held step's own guarantee).
        assert(e >= 0 and e <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max));
        if (e > cl) {
            // The held linked detector has crossed the ceiling; the single gain is ceil/e, in (0, 1) since 1 <= ceil < e. e > cl >= 1, so the divide is always safe (the zero-envelope case the gate faced cannot arise here).
            const ml: i64 = if (xl < 0) -@as(i64, xl) else @as(i64, xl); // |left| — safe in i64 (|sample_min| = 32768 fits)
            const mr: i64 = if (xr < 0) -@as(i64, xr) else @as(i64, xr); // |right|
            // ONE shared gain ceil/e across both channels: m-ceil <= 32768-32767 fits i64; divTrunc keeps each quotient strictly below its m (ceil < e).
            const nl: i64 = @divTrunc(ml * cl, e);
            const nr: i64 = @divTrunc(mr * cl, e);
            // invariant: the shared gain reduces, never boosts, and never below zero, on each channel -- ceil < e makes ceil/e < 1, so a smoothed limiter only ever leans down.
            assert(nl <= ml and nl >= 0 and nr <= mr and nr >= 0);
            const yl: i16 = @intCast(if (xl < 0) -nl else nl); // safe: 0 <= nl <= ml <= sample_max
            const yr: i16 = @intCast(if (xr < 0) -nr else nr);
            sc.left.buf[start + i] = yl;
            sc.right.buf[start + i] = yr;
            // invariant: each written sample holds its input's sign (magnitude only shrank), zero being sign-neutral.
            assert((xl >= 0 and yl >= 0) or (xl <= 0 and yl <= 0));
            assert((xr >= 0 and yr >= 0) or (xr <= 0 and yr <= 0));
        }
        // held envelope within the ceiling: the pair plays -- both left exactly as they were; the hold keeping the pinned peak above the ceiling is what keeps the gain reduction engaged through a brief duck rather than pumping.
    }

    // invariant: a limiter changes sample values only -- each channel holds its starting length and the channels leave balanced, the stereo image aligned in time.
    assert(sc.left.len == len and sc.right.len == len);
    assert(sc.left.len <= sc.left.buf.len and sc.right.len <= sc.right.buf.len);
}

/// Limit `count` sample-pairs starting at `start` in the stereo master `sc`, the one gain reduction following a LINKED HELD envelope that begins FROM SILENCE (a fresh shared env of 0 with the hold unarmed) -- one implementation
/// shared with the carried form, so the two cannot drift. Refuses BadCeiling, BadCoeff, BadHold, and BadRange before any write.
pub fn stereo_limit_hold_follow(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    ceil: i32,
    attack_num: u32,
    attack_den: u32,
    release_num: u32,
    release_den: u32,
    hold_samples: u32,
) StereoLimitEnvHoldError!void {
    var env: i64 = 0;
    var hold_left: u32 = 0;
    return stereo_limit_hold_follow_carry(sc, start, count, &env, &hold_left, ceil, attack_num, attack_den, release_num, release_den, hold_samples);
}

// --- selftest: THE ZERO-HOLD LAW -- a hold of zero reduces to ALES163's stereo_limit_env byte for byte on both; THE LINKED-GAIN-OVER-TIME LAW -- a left-always-peak master has left' == ALES59's mono limit_env_hold(left), the
//     right scaled by the same fraction, the independent reduction differing; THE IMAGE LAW -- no magnitude expands, no sign flips, an out-of-phase pair returns still out of phase; THE KNOB / NO-PUMP LAWS -- below the ceiling the
//     identity, a nonzero hold keeping the gain reduction engaged across a duck; THE CARRY LAW -- a span in two pieces (split mid-hold) equals the whole once on both; THE SUB-CEILING / SILENCE / BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn abs16(v: i16) i32 {
    return if (v < 0) -@as(i32, v) else @as(i32, v);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE ZERO-HOLD LAW -- the crux link. With hold_samples == 0 the linked held follower's fall branch never pins, so every pair moves through ALES165's step exactly as ALES163's un-held stereo_limit_env does: the held
    //    stereo limiter with no hold IS the ALES163 stereo limiter, byte-for-byte by construction. Proven by running BOTH tools on the same master and demanding equality on both channels (the no-hold limit).
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 3000, -3000, 500, -20000, 20000, -400, 6000, -6000 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 1200, 500, 2000, -700, 100, -1500, 1500 };
        var held = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&held, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_limit_hold_follow(&held, 0, 8, 1500, 1, 3, 1, 5, 0); // hold of zero, real attack/release
        var plain = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&plain, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_limit_env.stereo_limit_follow(&plain, 0, 8, 1500, 1, 3, 1, 5); // the proven ALES163 un-held linked limiter
        if (!eq(held.left.samples(), plain.left.samples())) return error.ZeroHoldLeftNotPlain;
        if (!eq(held.right.samples(), plain.right.samples())) return error.ZeroHoldRightNotPlain;
    }

    // 2. THE LINKED-GAIN-OVER-TIME LAW -- the stereo crux. A master where the LEFT always holds the peak (|left| >= |right| everywhere) makes the shared held envelope equal the mono held envelope of the left, so left' equals
    //    ALES59's mono limit_env_hold(left) byte for byte, and the right is scaled by the SAME fraction ceil/e -- a different, image-preserving result than limiting the right on its own.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 8000, -4000, 500, -2000, 6000, -300, 6000, -6000 }; // |left| >= |right| everywhere; the left holds the linked peak
        const rr = [_]i16{ 2000, -1000, 100, -1500, 700, -60, 1500, 1500 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_limit_hold_follow(&sc, 0, 8, 1000, 1, 1, 1, 5, 2); // instant attack, release 1:5, hold 2

        // the peak-holding left equals ALES59's mono limit_env_hold of it, proven against the mono tool directly.
        var mono_l = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mono_l, 0, &ll);
        try limit_env_hold.limit_hold_follow(&mono_l, 0, 8, 1000, 1, 1, 1, 5, 2);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), mono_l.samples())) return error.PeakLeftNotMono;

        // the INDEPENDENT reduction genuinely differs -- mono limit_env_hold of the right alone follows the right's own (quieter) held envelope, an image-shifting result.
        var mono_r = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mono_r, 0, &rr);
        try limit_env_hold.limit_hold_follow(&mono_r, 0, 8, 1000, 1, 1, 1, 5, 2);
        if (eq(sc.right.samples(), mono_r.samples())) return error.IndependentDidNotDiffer;
    }

    // 3. THE IMAGE LAW -- the shared gain preserves the L:R ratio. Over a mixed master no output magnitude exceeds its input's and no sign flips; and an out-of-phase pair (right = −left) comes back STILL out of phase, both
    //    scaled by the identical time-smoothed-and-held fraction.
    {
        const il = [_]i16{ 900, -700, 15000, -400, 9000, -9000 };
        const ir = [_]i16{ -400, 600, -3000, 200, -700, 700 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &il, &ir);
        try stereo_limit_hold_follow(&sc, 0, 6, 1000, 1, 2, 1, 6, 2);
        const l = sc.left.samples();
        const r = sc.right.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 6) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(l[i]) > abs16(il[i]) or abs16(r[i]) > abs16(ir[i])) return error.MagnitudeExpanded;
            if ((il[i] > 0 and l[i] < 0) or (il[i] < 0 and l[i] > 0)) return error.LeftSignFlipped;
            if ((ir[i] > 0 and r[i] < 0) or (ir[i] < 0 and r[i] > 0)) return error.RightSignFlipped;
        }

        var op = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&op, &[_]i16{ 6000, -8000, 12000, -4000 }, &[_]i16{ -6000, 8000, -12000, 4000 });
        try stereo_limit_hold_follow(&op, 0, 4, 1000, 1, 2, 1, 6, 2);
        const ol = op.left.samples();
        const orr = op.right.samples();
        var j: u32 = 0;
        while (j < 4) : (j += 1) {
            if (@as(i32, orr[j]) != -@as(i32, ol[j])) return error.OutOfPhaseNotPreserved;
        }
    }

    // 4a. BELOW THE CEILING IS THE IDENTITY on both -- a master whose every linked peak sits within the ceiling never drives the held envelope above it, so the whole master passes byte for byte regardless of smoothing or hold.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 500, -900, 1000, -1000, 250, -500 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 600, -700, 200, -50, 100 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_limit_hold_follow(&sc, 0, 6, 1000, 1, 4, 1, 8, 4); // ceiling at the loudest peak, real attack/release, hold 4
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &ll) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &rr)) return error.SubCeilingNotIdentity;
    }

    // 4b. A NONZERO HOLD KEEPS THE GAIN REDUCTION ENGAGED ACROSS A DUCK -- a loud pair, one quiet duck below the ceiling, then loud again, the left holding the linked peak. With INSTANT attack and release and NO hold the duck
    //     drops the envelope within the ceiling and passes untouched (the reduction released -- pump). With a hold that spans the duck the pinned peak stays above the ceiling so the reduction stays engaged and the duck is
    //     attenuated. So the held magnitude is no LARGER than the un-held one at every sample (the hold only keeps the reduction ENGAGED, monotone), and strictly QUIETER at the duck (the pumping avoided).
    {
        const dl = [_]i16{ 15000, 900, 15000 }; // loud, a quiet duck below the ceiling, loud again — the left holds the linked peak
        const dr = [_]i16{ -400, 50, -400 };
        const ceil: i32 = 1000;
        var none = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&none, &dl, &dr);
        try stereo_limit_hold_follow(&none, 0, 3, ceil, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0); // instant attack, instant release, no hold
        var held = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&held, &dl, &dr);
        try stereo_limit_hold_follow(&held, 0, 3, ceil, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1); // same, hold of 1 spans the single duck sample
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 3) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(held.left.samples()[i]) > abs16(none.left.samples()[i])) return error.HoldLimitedLess;
        }
        // the duck sample (index 1): the held limiter reads the pinned peak above the ceiling and attenuates it, the un-held limiter has already dropped within the ceiling and passes it -- the reduction held engaged through the duck.
        if (abs16(held.left.samples()[1]) >= abs16(none.left.samples()[1])) return error.HoldDidNotSurvive;
    }

    // 5. THE CARRY LAW -- the shared state carries, env AND hold_left both -- so limiting a span in two pieces (the split falling MID-HOLD, the second continuing the first's ending shared envelope and countdown) equals limiting
    //    the whole span once, byte for byte on BOTH channels.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 2000, -14000, 14000, -2000, 9000, -9000, 6000, -6000 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 6000, -1000, 2000, -700, 800, -1500, 1500 };
        var whole = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&whole, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_limit_hold_follow(&whole, 0, 8, 1500, 1, 3, 1, 5, 2);

        var split = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&split, &ll, &rr);
        var env: i64 = 0;
        var hold_left: u32 = 0;
        try stereo_limit_hold_follow_carry(&split, 0, 3, &env, &hold_left, 1500, 1, 3, 1, 5, 2); // first piece — split at index 3 mid-hold
        try stereo_limit_hold_follow_carry(&split, 3, 5, &env, &hold_left, 1500, 1, 3, 1, 5, 2); // second continues env AND hold_left
        if (!eq(split.left.samples(), whole.left.samples())) return error.CarryLeftNotEqual;
        if (!eq(split.right.samples(), whole.right.samples())) return error.CarryRightNotEqual;
    }

    // 6a. THE SILENCE / BALANCE / LENGTH LAW -- an all-silent master limits to zero on both; the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 }, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 });
        try stereo_limit_hold_follow(&sc, 0, 4, 1000, 1, 4, 1, 8, 3);
        const zeros = [_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 };
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &zeros) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &zeros)) return error.SilenceWrong;

        var bc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&bc, &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 200, -9000, 100 }, &[_]i16{ -400, 3000, -700, 5000, -50 });
        try stereo_limit_hold_follow(&bc, 0, 5, 1000, 1, 4, 1, 8, 3);
        if (bc.left.len != bc.right.len) return error.Unbalanced;
        if (bc.left.len != 5) return error.LengthChanged;
    }

    // 6b. THE ATOMICITY LAW -- a zero ceiling, an over-rail ceiling, an illegal coefficient, a hold past max_clip, and an out-of-range span each refuse by name with BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced.
    {
        const tl = [_]i16{ 5, -6, 7 };
        const tr = [_]i16{ -7, 6, -5 };

        var a = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&a, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_limit_hold_follow(&a, 0, 3, 0, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.ZeroCeilSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCeiling) return error.WrongZeroCeil;
        if (!eq(a.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(a.right.samples(), &tr) or a.left.len != a.right.len) return error.ZeroCeilRefusalMutated;

        var b = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&b, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_limit_hold_follow(&b, 0, 3, @as(i32, timeline.sample_max) + 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.OverRailSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCeiling) return error.WrongOverRail;
        if (!eq(b.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(b.right.samples(), &tr) or b.left.len != b.right.len) return error.OverRailRefusalMutated;

        var c = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&c, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_limit_hold_follow(&c, 0, 3, 1500, 5, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.BadAttackSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCoeff) return error.WrongBadAttack;
        if (!eq(c.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(c.right.samples(), &tr) or c.left.len != c.right.len) return error.AttackRefusalMutated;

        var d = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&d, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_limit_hold_follow(&d, 0, 3, 1500, 1, 4, 1, 8, timeline.max_clip + 1)) |_| return error.BadHoldSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadHold) return error.WrongBadHold;
        if (!eq(d.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(d.right.samples(), &tr) or d.left.len != d.right.len) return error.HoldRefusalMutated;

        var f = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&f, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_limit_hold_follow(&f, 1, 3, 1500, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(f.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(f.right.samples(), &tr) or f.left.len != f.right.len) return error.BadRangeRefusalMutated;
    }

    // 6c. THE DEGENERATE LAW -- count = 0 (an empty span in the middle) is the identity on both channels; no pair to limit.
    {
        var noop = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&noop, &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 4000 }, &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 });
        try stereo_limit_hold_follow(&noop, 1, 0, 1000, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2); // empty span — identity
        if (!eq(noop.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 4000 })) return error.ZeroCountLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(noop.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 })) return error.ZeroCountRightChanged;
    }

    // silence the unused import in builds that trim it -- limit is named in the doc lineage; touch it so the reference stays honest.
    _ = limit.limit_version;

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-limit-env-hold: the ATTACK/HOLD/RELEASE BRICKWALL LIMITER CARRIED INTO STEREO — the LINKED HELD envelope driving ONE LINKED ceiling over time, the fourth spender of the held time base, the stereo " ++
        "held dynamics QUARTET made whole. Limit a span of sample-PAIRS in a master to a magnitude ceiling, the one gain reduction following the shared LINKED HELD envelope (max(|left|, |right|), attack while rising and " ++
        "rearming the shared hold, the peak pinned while the countdown lasts, release once it expires): within the ceiling both pass byte for byte, above it the single gain ceil/e (in (0, 1)) is applied to both raw samples — " ++
        "the compressor at an infinite ratio, the target pinned to the ceiling. ALES163 built the un-held stereo breathing limiter; ALES165 built the linked held time base; this fuses them — one detector, one hold, one " ++
        "ceiling, the image held the whole time, the hold keeping the gain reduction engaged across a duck rather than pumping the field. THE ZERO-HOLD LAW (the crux link): a hold of zero reduces to ALES163's stereo_limit_env " ++
        "byte for byte on both. THE LINKED-GAIN-OVER-TIME LAW: a left-always-peak master has left' == ALES59's mono limit_env_hold(left), the right scaled by the same fraction, the independent per-channel limit_env_hold " ++
        "genuinely differing. THE IMAGE LAW: the shared gain preserves the L:R ratio, an out-of-phase pair returns still out of phase, no magnitude expands, no sign flips. THE KNOB / NO-PUMP LAWS: below the ceiling the " ++
        "identity everywhere, a nonzero hold keeping the gain reduction engaged across a brief duck (it does not pump, the knob monotone). THE CARRY LAW: a span limited in two pieces (the split mid-hold) equals the whole once " ++
        "on both, env and hold_left carrying. THE SUB-CEILING / SILENCE / BALANCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a master whose linked held envelope never crosses the ceiling passing byte for byte, silence staying silence, " ++
        "left.len == right.len after, each of the four refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, count = 0 the identity on both. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips carrying one shared i64 envelope and one shared " ++
        "u32 hold countdown, at most one multiply-then-divide for the step plus one per channel for the gain (in i64, no overflow), the gain ceil/e in (0, 1); the attack, release, and hold per sample index; no lookahead; no " ++
        "real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
